{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:10949035 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:10949035 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"10949035","source":"MED","pmid":"10949035","doi":"10.1016/s1097-2765(05)00006-7","title":"Activation of human liver glycogen phosphorylase by alteration of the secondary structure and packing of the catalytic core.","authorString":"Rath VL, Ammirati M, LeMotte PK, Fennell KF, Mansour MN, Danley DE, Hynes TR, Schulte GK, Wasilko DJ, Pandit J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Rath VL","lastName":"Rath","firstName":"V L","initials":"VL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Exploratory Medicinal Sciences, Global Research and Development, Groton Laboratories, Pfizer, Inc, Connecticut 06340, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ammirati M","lastName":"Ammirati","firstName":"M","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"LeMotte PK","lastName":"LeMotte","firstName":"P K","initials":"PK"},{"fullName":"Fennell KF","lastName":"Fennell","firstName":"K F","initials":"KF"},{"fullName":"Mansour MN","lastName":"Mansour","firstName":"M N","initials":"MN"},{"fullName":"Danley DE","lastName":"Danley","firstName":"D E","initials":"DE"},{"fullName":"Hynes TR","lastName":"Hynes","firstName":"T R","initials":"TR"},{"fullName":"Schulte GK","lastName":"Schulte","firstName":"G K","initials":"GK"},{"fullName":"Wasilko DJ","lastName":"Wasilko","firstName":"D J","initials":"DJ"},{"fullName":"Pandit J","lastName":"Pandit","firstName":"J","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6128-7779"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6128-7779"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["related_data","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"6","journalIssueId":1052854,"dateOfPublication":"2000 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2000,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Mol Cell","title":"Molecular cell","essn":"1097-4164","issn":"1097-2765","nlmid":"9802571","isoabbreviation":"Mol Cell"},"printPublicationDate":"2000-07-01"},"pubYear":"2000","pageInfo":"139-148","abstractText":"Glycogen phosphorylases catalyze the breakdown of glycogen to glucose-1-phosphate, which enters glycolysis to fulfill the energetic requirements of the organism. Maintaining control of blood glucose levels is critical in minimizing the debilitating effects of diabetes, making liver glycogen phosphorylase a potential therapeutic target. To support inhibitor design, we determined the crystal structures of the active and inactive forms of human liver glycogen phosphorylase a. During activation, forty residues of the catalytic site undergo order/disorder transitions, changes in secondary structure, or packing to reorganize the catalytic site for substrate binding and catalysis. Knowing the inactive and active conformations of the liver enzyme and how each differs from its counterpart in muscle phosphorylase provides the basis for designing inhibitors that bind preferentially to the inactive conformation of the liver isozyme.","affiliation":"Exploratory Medicinal Sciences, Global Research and Development, Groton Laboratories, Pfizer, Inc, Connecticut 06340, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Muscles","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EN","qualifierName":"enzymology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Liver","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"EN","qualifierName":"enzymology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Rabbits"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Diabetes Mellitus","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTop